                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Object-Oriented Programming</h2>
                    <p><strong>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</strong> is a programming paradigm that organizes code around "objects" – bundles of data and behavior. C# is fundamentally an object-oriented language.</p>
                    
                    <p>In this lesson, you'll learn how to create your own types using classes, and understand the core concepts of OOP.</p>

                    <div class="oop-pillars">
                        <div class="pillar">
                            <span class="pillar-icon">📦</span>
                            <h4>Encapsulation</h4>
                            <p>Bundling data with methods that operate on it</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="pillar">
                            <span class="pillar-icon">🧬</span>
                            <h4>Inheritance</h4>
                            <p>Creating new classes based on existing ones</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="pillar">
                            <span class="pillar-icon">🎭</span>
                            <h4>Polymorphism</h4>
                            <p>Objects taking many forms</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="pillar">
                            <span class="pillar-icon">🎨</span>
                            <h4>Abstraction</h4>
                            <p>Hiding complexity, showing essentials</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Classes and Objects</h2>
                    <p>A <strong>class</strong> is a blueprint for creating objects. An <strong>object</strong> is an instance of a class.</p>

                    <div class="class-diagram">
                        <div class="diagram-class">
                            <div class="diagram-header">Class: Person (Blueprint)</div>
                            <div class="diagram-section">
                                <strong>Fields:</strong>
                                <span>Name, Age, Email</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="diagram-section">
                                <strong>Methods:</strong>
                                <span>SayHello(), HaveBirthday()</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="diagram-arrow">↓ creates</div>
                        <div class="diagram-objects">
                            <div class="diagram-object">
                                <div class="object-header">alice</div>
                                <span>Name: "Alice"</span>
                                <span>Age: 25</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="diagram-object">
                                <div class="object-header">bob</div>
                                <span>Name: "Bob"</span>
                                <span>Age: 30</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Define a class
class Person
{
    // Fields (data)
    public string Name;
    public int Age;
    
    // Method (behavior)
    public void SayHello()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Hi, I'm {Name} and I'm {Age} years old.");
    }
}

// Create objects (instances)
Person alice = new Person();
alice.Name = "Alice";
alice.Age = 25;
alice.SayHello();  // Hi, I'm Alice and I'm 25 years old.

Person bob = new Person();
bob.Name = "Bob";
bob.Age = 30;
bob.SayHello();  // Hi, I'm Bob and I'm 30 years old.</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Fields and Properties</h2>
                    <p><strong>Fields</strong> store data directly. <strong>Properties</strong> provide controlled access to data with getters and setters.</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class BankAccount
{
    // Field (private - internal use only)
    private decimal _balance;
    
    // Property with getter and setter
    public decimal Balance
    {
        get { return _balance; }
        set 
        { 
            if (value &gt;= 0)
                _balance = value; 
        }
    }
    
    // Auto-implemented property (most common)
    public string AccountHolder { get; set; }
    
    // Read-only property (no setter)
    public string AccountNumber { get; }
    
    // Property with private setter
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; private set; }
}</code></pre>

                    <h3>Auto-Implemented Properties</h3>
                    <p>For simple cases, use auto-implemented properties – the compiler creates the backing field for you:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class Product
{
    // Auto-implemented properties (recommended)
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public decimal Price { get; set; }
    public int Stock { get; set; }
    
    // With default value
    public bool IsAvailable { get; set; } = true;
    
    // Read-only (set only in constructor)
    public int Id { get; }
}</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box success">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Best Practice</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Always use properties instead of public fields. Properties allow you to add validation, logging, or change the implementation later without breaking code.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Constructors</h2>
                    <p>A <strong>constructor</strong> is a special method that runs when an object is created. Use it to initialize the object:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
    
    // Constructor
    public Person(string name, int age)
    {
        Name = name;
        Age = age;
    }
    
    public void SayHello()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Hi, I'm {Name}!");
    }
}

// Now we must provide values when creating
Person alice = new Person("Alice", 25);
alice.SayHello();  // Hi, I'm Alice!

// This would be an error now:
// Person bob = new Person();  // Error: no parameterless constructor</code></pre>

                    <h3>Constructor Overloading</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class Product
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public decimal Price { get; set; }
    public string Category { get; set; }
    
    // Parameterless constructor
    public Product()
    {
        Name = "Unknown";
        Price = 0;
        Category = "General";
    }
    
    // Constructor with parameters
    public Product(string name, decimal price)
    {
        Name = name;
        Price = price;
        Category = "General";
    }
    
    // Constructor calling another constructor
    public Product(string name, decimal price, string category) 
        : this(name, price)  // Call the two-parameter constructor
    {
        Category = category;
    }
}

// Different ways to create
Product p1 = new Product();
Product p2 = new Product("Laptop", 999.99m);
Product p3 = new Product("Phone", 599.99m, "Electronics");</code></pre>

                    <h3>Primary Constructors (C# 12+)</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Modern, concise syntax
class Person(string name, int age)
{
    public string Name { get; } = name;
    public int Age { get; } = age;
    
    public void SayHello() =&gt; Console.WriteLine($"Hi, I'm {Name}!");
}

Person alice = new Person("Alice", 25);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Access Modifiers</h2>
                    <p>Access modifiers control who can access your class members:</p>

                    <div class="access-table">
                        <div class="access-row header">
                            <span>Modifier</span>
                            <span>Accessible From</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="access-row">
                            <code>public</code>
                            <span>Anywhere</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="access-row">
                            <code>private</code>
                            <span>Same class only</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="access-row">
                            <code>protected</code>
                            <span>Same class and derived classes</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="access-row">
                            <code>internal</code>
                            <span>Same assembly (project)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="access-row">
                            <code>protected internal</code>
                            <span>Same assembly OR derived classes</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="access-row">
                            <code>private protected</code>
                            <span>Same assembly AND derived classes</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class BankAccount
{
    // Private - only this class can access
    private decimal _balance;
    
    // Public - anyone can access
    public string AccountHolder { get; set; }
    
    // Protected - this class and subclasses
    protected string AccountType { get; set; }
    
    // Private method
    private void LogTransaction(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[LOG] {message}");
    }
    
    // Public method (uses private members internally)
    public void Deposit(decimal amount)
    {
        _balance += amount;
        LogTransaction($"Deposited {amount:C}");
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Methods in Classes</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class Calculator
{
    // Instance method - operates on object data
    public int Value { get; set; }
    
    public void Add(int number)
    {
        Value += number;
    }
    
    public void Reset()
    {
        Value = 0;
    }
    
    // Method that returns a value
    public int GetSquare()
    {
        return Value * Value;
    }
}

Calculator calc = new Calculator();
calc.Value = 5;
calc.Add(3);
Console.WriteLine(calc.Value);  // 8
Console.WriteLine(calc.GetSquare());  // 64</code></pre>

                    <h3>The this Keyword</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
    
    // 'this' refers to the current object
    public Person(string name, int age)
    {
        this.Name = name;  // this.Name is the property
        this.Age = age;    // 'name' is the parameter
    }
    
    // Returning 'this' for method chaining
    public Person WithName(string name)
    {
        this.Name = name;
        return this;
    }
    
    public Person WithAge(int age)
    {
        this.Age = age;
        return this;
    }
}

// Method chaining (fluent interface)
Person person = new Person("", 0)
    .WithName("Alice")
    .WithAge(25);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Static Members</h2>
                    <p><strong>Static</strong> members belong to the class itself, not to any instance:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class MathHelper
{
    // Static field - shared by all
    public static double Pi = 3.14159;
    
    // Static method - no instance needed
    public static int Add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    public static double CircleArea(double radius)
    {
        return Pi * radius * radius;
    }
}

// Use with class name, no 'new' needed
Console.WriteLine(MathHelper.Pi);           // 3.14159
Console.WriteLine(MathHelper.Add(5, 3));    // 8
Console.WriteLine(MathHelper.CircleArea(5)); // 78.54...</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class Player
{
    // Static field - tracks total across all instances
    public static int TotalPlayers { get; private set; } = 0;
    
    // Instance properties
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Score { get; set; }
    
    public Player(string name)
    {
        Name = name;
        TotalPlayers++;  // Increment when new player created
    }
}

Player p1 = new Player("Alice");
Player p2 = new Player("Bob");
Console.WriteLine(Player.TotalPlayers);  // 2</code></pre>

                    <div class="comparison-box">
                        <div class="comparison-item">
                            <h4>Instance Members</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Belong to each object</li>
                                <li>Need an instance to access</li>
                                <li>Each object has its own copy</li>
                                <li><code>person.Name</code></li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-item">
                            <h4>Static Members</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Belong to the class</li>
                                <li>Access via class name</li>
                                <li>Shared by all instances</li>
                                <li><code>Math.PI</code></li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Records (C# 9+)</h2>
                    <p>For simple data objects, use <strong>records</strong> – they're immutable by default and come with useful features:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Record declaration (super concise!)
record Person(string Name, int Age);

// Create instances
var alice = new Person("Alice", 25);
var bob = new Person("Bob", 30);

// Access properties
Console.WriteLine(alice.Name);  // Alice

// Records are immutable - create new with 'with'
var olderAlice = alice with { Age = 26 };

// Value-based equality (not reference)
var alice2 = new Person("Alice", 25);
Console.WriteLine(alice == alice2);  // true (same values!)

// Nice string representation
Console.WriteLine(alice);  // Person { Name = Alice, Age = 25 }</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>When to Use Records</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Use records for DTOs (Data Transfer Objects), configuration, or any type where you care about the data, not the identity.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Complete Example</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class BankAccount
{
    // Static member
    private static int _nextAccountNumber = 1000;
    
    // Properties
    public int AccountNumber { get; }
    public string Owner { get; set; }
    public decimal Balance { get; private set; }
    
    // Constructor
    public BankAccount(string owner, decimal initialDeposit = 0)
    {
        AccountNumber = _nextAccountNumber++;
        Owner = owner;
        Balance = initialDeposit;
    }
    
    // Methods
    public void Deposit(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount &gt; 0)
        {
            Balance += amount;
            Console.WriteLine($"Deposited {amount:C}. New balance: {Balance:C}");
        }
    }
    
    public bool Withdraw(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; amount &lt;= Balance)
        {
            Balance -= amount;
            Console.WriteLine($"Withdrew {amount:C}. New balance: {Balance:C}");
            return true;
        }
        Console.WriteLine("Insufficient funds!");
        return false;
    }
    
    public override string ToString()
    {
        return $"Account #{AccountNumber}: {Owner} - Balance: {Balance:C}";
    }
}

// Usage
var account = new BankAccount("Alice", 1000);
account.Deposit(500);     // Deposited $500.00. New balance: $1,500.00
account.Withdraw(200);    // Withdrew $200.00. New balance: $1,300.00
Console.WriteLine(account); // Account #1000: Alice - Balance: $1,300.00</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>A class is a blueprint; an object is an instance of a class.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use properties instead of public fields for data encapsulation.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Constructors initialize objects when they're created.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Access modifiers (public, private) control visibility.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Static members belong to the class, not instances.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

